-- ====== NEET PG — ANATOMY (42 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','medium','The nerve most commonly injured in fracture of surgical neck of humerus is:','["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]',0,'Axillary nerve winds around surgical neck of humerus — most vulnerable in fractures here. Causes deltoid paralysis and patch of anaesthesia over deltoid.','Harrison''s / First Aid',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','hard','In Erb''s palsy, the arm assumes the "waiter''s tip" position because of paralysis of:','["C5, C6 roots — abductors, lateral rotators, supinators","C7, C8 roots","C8, T1 roots","C6, C7 roots"]',0,'Erb''s palsy: C5-C6 injury. Paralysis of deltoid, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, biceps, supinator → arm adducted, medially rotated, pronated = waiter''s tip.','Harrison''s',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Lower Limb','medium','Foot drop is caused by damage to which nerve?','["Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Femoral nerve"]',0,'Common peroneal (fibular) nerve injury causes foot drop — loss of dorsiflexion and eversion.','Harrison''s',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Lower Limb','hard','The nerve that supplies the skin over the medial aspect of the leg and foot is:','["Saphenous nerve (femoral branch)","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve"]',0,'Saphenous nerve (longest branch of femoral nerve) supplies medial leg and foot skin.','Grants Atlas',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Head and Neck','medium','Which cranial nerve is tested by the gag reflex?','["Glossopharyngeal (IX) — afferent; Vagus (X) — efferent","Vagus (X) both","Trigeminal (V)","Facial (VII)"]',0,'Gag reflex: CN IX is afferent (posterior pharynx); CN X is efferent (pharyngeal muscles).','Grays Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Head and Neck','hard','Chorda tympani joins lingual nerve to carry:','["Taste from anterior 2/3 tongue + preganglionic parasympathetics to submandibular ganglion","Motor to tongue muscles","Sensation from posterior tongue","Taste from posterior tongue"]',0,'Chorda tympani (branch of VII): taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue; preganglionic PS fibres to submandibular and sublingual glands.','Grays Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Thorax','medium','The phrenic nerve originates mainly from:','["C4 (with contributions from C3, C5)","C3 only","C5 only","C3-C6"]',0,'Phrenic nerve: C3,4,5 (keeps the diaphragm alive — "C3,4,5 keep the diaphragm alive").','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Thorax','hard','Coronary artery most commonly affected in MI in India:','["Left anterior descending (LAD)","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex","Left main"]',0,'LAD supplies anterior LV wall and interventricular septum — most commonly affected in MI worldwide.','Harrison''s',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','medium','Portal vein is formed by the union of:','["Superior mesenteric vein + splenic vein","Inferior mesenteric + superior mesenteric","Splenic + left gastric + inferior mesenteric","Portal + superior mesenteric"]',0,'Portal vein = SMV + splenic vein, behind the neck of pancreas.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','hard','McBurney''s point is located at:','["Junction of medial 2/3 and lateral 1/3 of line from umbilicus to ASIS","Right iliac fossa","Midpoint between umbilicus and ASIS","Left iliac fossa"]',0,'McBurney''s point: 1/3 of the way from ASIS to umbilicus — maximum tenderness in appendicitis.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Pelvis','medium','The levator ani muscle forms the floor of the:','["Pelvic diaphragm","Urogenital diaphragm","Pelvic sidewall","Abdominal wall"]',0,'Levator ani (with coccygeus) forms the pelvic diaphragm — floor of the pelvic cavity.','Grays Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','medium','The primary motor cortex is located in:','["Precentral gyrus (area 4)","Postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex","Temporal lobe"]',0,'Primary motor cortex = precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4), anterior to central sulcus.','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','hard','Broca''s area is responsible for speech production and is located in:','["Inferior frontal gyrus (areas 44, 45) — usually left hemisphere","Wernicke''s area","Angular gyrus","Superior temporal gyrus"]',0,'Broca''s area: pars triangularis and pars opercularis of inferior frontal gyrus — expressive/motor speech.','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','medium','Anatomical snuffbox boundaries include all EXCEPT:','["Extensor carpi ulnaris","Abductor pollicis longus","Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus"]',0,'Anatomical snuffbox: lateral = APL + EPB, medial = EPL. Extensor carpi ulnaris is NOT a boundary.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Embryology','medium','The foramen ovale in the heart closes:','["After birth due to increased left atrial pressure","Before birth","At 6 weeks gestation","Never in 30% of population"]',0,'Foramen ovale closes functionally at birth (increased LA pressure) and anatomically by 3 months. Remains patent in ~25-30% as PFO.','Langman''s Embryology',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Embryology','hard','Truncus arteriosus gives rise to:','["Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk","Both ventricles","Atria only","All four chambers"]',0,'Truncus arteriosus → ascending aorta + pulmonary trunk after neural crest cell-derived septum divides it.','Langman''s Embryology',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Head and Neck','medium','The parotid gland is pierced by:','["Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Vagus nerve"]',0,'Facial nerve (VII) branches pass through the parotid gland — risk of injury during parotidectomy.','Grays Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Lower Limb','medium','The femoral triangle is bounded by:','["Inguinal ligament (above), Sartorius (lateral), Adductor longus (medial)","Inguinal ligament, Gracilis, Rectus femoris","Inguinal ligament, Iliopsoas, Sartorius","Femoral sheath boundaries only"]',0,'Femoral triangle: roof = inguinal ligament; lateral = sartorius; medial = adductor longus; floor = iliopsoas/pectineus.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Thorax','medium','Aortic stenosis murmur is best heard at:','["Right 2nd intercostal space (aortic area)","Apex","Lower left sternal border","Left 2nd ICS"]',0,'Aortic stenosis: harsh ejection systolic murmur, best heard at right 2nd ICS, radiates to carotids.','Harrison''s',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','medium','The common bile duct passes through which part of the duodenum?','["Second part (descending)","First part","Third part","Fourth part"]',0,'CBD passes through the head of pancreas and opens into the 2nd part (descending) of duodenum at major duodenal papilla.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','hard','The carpal tunnel transmits all of the following EXCEPT:','["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Flexor pollicis longus tendon","Flexor digitorum superficialis tendons"]',0,'Carpal tunnel contains median nerve + 9 flexor tendons (FPL×1, FDS×4, FDP×4). Ulnar nerve passes in Guyon''s canal — NOT carpal tunnel.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','hard','Internal capsule is supplied mainly by:','["Lenticulostriate arteries (medial striate)","Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Basilar artery"]',0,'Internal capsule: mainly lenticulostriate arteries — branches of MCA and anterior choroidal artery. Rupture → contralateral hemiplegia.','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Embryology','medium','Which structure divides the embryonic gut tube into esophagus and trachea?','["Tracheoesophageal septum","Lateral plate mesoderm","Notochord","Intermediate mesoderm"]',0,'Tracheoesophageal (TE) septum grows downward to separate the foregut into trachea (anterior) and esophagus (posterior).','Langman''s Embryology',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Pelvis','hard','The ureter is crossed by the uterine artery at:','["Lateral fornix of vagina (2 cm lateral to cervix)","Brim of pelvis","Bladder base","Ureteral orifice"]',0,'"Water under the bridge": uterine artery crosses over ureter at the lateral fornix — risk of ligation in hysterectomy.','Grays Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Head and Neck','hard','Superior thyroid artery is the first branch of:','["External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","Common carotid artery","Subclavian artery"]',0,'External carotid artery: first branch = superior thyroid artery. Mnemonic: Some Anatomists Love Freaking Out Poor Medical Students.','Grays Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','hard','Hilum of the kidney lies at the level of:','["L1 vertebra","L2","L3","T12"]',0,'Renal hilum is at L1 (transpyloric plane). Right kidney is slightly lower than left.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Lower Limb','hard','The popliteal artery divides into anterior and posterior tibial arteries at:','["Lower border of popliteus muscle","Upper border of popliteus","Knee joint level","Fibular neck"]',0,'Popliteal artery divides at the lower border of popliteus into anterior tibial (passes through interosseous membrane) and posterior tibial.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Thorax','hard','The azygos vein drains into:','["Superior vena cava (at T4 level)","Inferior vena cava","Right atrium directly","Left brachiocephalic vein"]',0,'Azygos vein arches over the root of right lung at T4 to drain into the SVC. It is the major collateral when IVC is obstructed.','Grays Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','medium','Winging of scapula is caused by damage to:','["Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) → serratus anterior","Accessory nerve","Axillary nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]',0,'Serratus anterior (holds scapula against chest wall) is supplied by long thoracic nerve — injury causes scapular winging.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','medium','The trigeminal nerve exits the brain at:','["Pons (ventrolateral surface)","Medulla","Midbrain","Cerebellopontine angle only"]',0,'Trigeminal nerve (CN V) has a motor and sensory root emerging from the lateral pons.','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Embryology','hard','The neural tube defect spina bifida occulta involves failure of fusion of:','["Vertebral arches (no neural tissue herniation)","Posterior neuropore","Anterior neuropore","Neural plate only"]',0,'Spina bifida occulta: posterior vertebral arches fail to fuse but meninges/cord don''t herniate; often asymptomatic.','Langman''s Embryology',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Pelvis','medium','Which lymph nodes drain the testes?','["Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes at L1","Inguinal lymph nodes","External iliac","Internal iliac"]',0,'Testes drain to para-aortic nodes (embryological origin from retroperitoneum). Scrotal skin drains to inguinal nodes.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Head and Neck','medium','Mastication muscles are all innervated by:','["Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]',0,'Muscles of mastication (temporalis, masseter, medial/lateral pterygoids) — all V3 (mandibular nerve).','Grays Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','medium','The hepatoduodenal ligament is part of the lesser omentum and contains:','["Portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct","Only bile duct","Hepatic vein + portal vein","Splenic artery + portal vein"]',0,'Hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of lesser omentum): portal vein (posterior), hepatic artery proper (left), bile duct (right) = portal triad.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','medium','The medial epicondyle of humerus gives attachment to:','["Common flexor origin (pronator teres, FCR, PL, FCU, FDS)","Common extensor origin","Biceps tendon","Triceps tendon"]',0,'Medial epicondyle = common flexor origin. Lateral epicondyle = common extensor origin.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','hard','The subthalamic nucleus lesion causes:','["Hemiballismus (contralateral)","Tremor at rest","Chorea","Bradykinesia"]',0,'Subthalamic nucleus (Luys body) lesion → hemiballismus: wild, flinging movements of contralateral limbs.','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Thorax','medium','Structures passing through the esophageal hiatus (T10) include:','["Esophagus + vagus nerves (anterior and posterior trunks)","Aorta + thoracic duct","IVC","Esophagus only"]',0,'Esophageal hiatus (T10): esophagus + left gastric vessels + vagus nerves. Aortic hiatus = T12. IVC = T8.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Lower Limb','medium','Sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body and arises from:','["L4, L5, S1, S2, S3","L4, L5, S1, S2","L3, L4, L5","L5, S1, S2"]',0,'Sciatic nerve: L4, L5, S1, S2, S3 — formed in the sacral plexus; exits through greater sciatic foramen below piriformis.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Abdomen','hard','The transpyloric plane (L1) passes through all EXCEPT:','["Renal pelvis","Pylorus of stomach","Fundus of gallbladder","Hilum of kidney"]',0,'Transpyloric plane (L1): pylorus, hilum of kidney, fundus of gallbladder, neck of pancreas, SMA origin, duodenojejunal flexure. Renal PELVIS is below L1.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2024),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Pelvis','medium','The internal os of the uterus is at the level of:','["Isthmus (anatomical internal os)","External os","Cervical canal midpoint","Fundus"]',0,'Internal os = junction of body and cervix (isthmus). External os = opening into vagina. Important in incompetent os/cervical cerclage.','Grays Anatomy',2023),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Upper Limb','hard','Which muscle is responsible for extension at the wrist and all four medial fingers (MCP, PIP, DIP)?','["Extensor digitorum","Extensor indicis","Lumbrical muscles","Flexor digitorum profundus"]',0,'Extensor digitorum (supplied by posterior interosseous nerve — deep branch radial) extends wrist and MCP joints of medial 4 fingers.','Clinically Oriented Anatomy',2022),
('NEET PG','Anatomy','Neuroanatomy','medium','The lateral geniculate body receives input from which nerve?','["Optic tract (CN II)","Oculomotor nerve","Trochlear nerve","Optic radiation"]',0,'Lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of thalamus receives input from optic tract and relays via optic radiation to primary visual cortex (V1).','Clinical Neuroanatomy',2024),

-- ====== NEET PG — PHYSIOLOGY (42 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','medium','The resting membrane potential of a neuron is approximately:','["−70 mV","−90 mV","0 mV","−55 mV"]',0,'Resting membrane potential ≈ −70 mV (mainly due to K⁺ diffusion and Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump).','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','hard','The refractory period of a nerve during which a stronger stimulus can elicit an action potential is called:','["Relative refractory period","Absolute refractory period","Supernormal period","Subnormal period"]',0,'Relative refractory period: Na⁺ channels partially reset; threshold higher → stronger stimulus needed. Absolute: no AP possible.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','medium','Normal ESR (Westergren method) in adult males is:','["0-15 mm/hour","0-20 mm/hour","0-10 mm/hour","20-30 mm/hour"]',0,'ESR (Westergren): males 0-15 mm/hr, females 0-20 mm/hr.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','hard','Iron is absorbed mainly in which part of the GI tract?','["Duodenum and proximal jejunum","Ileum","Stomach","Large intestine"]',0,'Iron is absorbed as Fe²⁺ (ferrous) in duodenum and proximal jejunum via DMT-1 transporter.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','medium','Starling''s law of heart states that:','["Force of contraction increases with end-diastolic volume (preload)","HR increases with preload","BP is proportional to HR","Stroke volume decreases with preload"]',0,'Starling''s law (Frank-Starling): greater ventricular filling (preload) → greater force of contraction → greater stroke volume.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','hard','The JVP waveform: ''a'' wave is absent in:','["Atrial fibrillation","Complete heart block","Pulmonary hypertension","Tricuspid stenosis"]',0,'''a'' wave = atrial contraction. In AF, there is no coordinated atrial contraction → ''a'' wave absent.','Harrison''s',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Respiratory','medium','The normal PaO₂ in arterial blood at sea level is:','["95-100 mmHg","40 mmHg","150 mmHg","60 mmHg"]',0,'Normal arterial PaO₂ = 95-100 mmHg. PaCO₂ = 35-45 mmHg. Alveolar PO₂ ≈ 100 mmHg.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Respiratory','hard','FEV1/FVC ratio is decreased below 70% in:','["Obstructive lung disease (COPD, asthma)","Restrictive disease","Normal elderly","Pulmonary fibrosis"]',0,'FEV1/FVC < 70% = obstructive pattern (COPD, asthma). In restrictive disease both FEV1 and FVC decrease proportionally.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Renal','medium','Glucose is normally completely reabsorbed in the kidney at plasma glucose up to:','["180 mg/dL (renal threshold)","100 mg/dL","250 mg/dL","120 mg/dL"]',0,'Renal threshold for glucose = 180 mg/dL. Above this, glucose spills into urine (glycosuria).','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Renal','hard','Aldosterone acts on the kidney to:','["Increase Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ excretion in collecting duct","Decrease Na⁺ reabsorption","Increase K⁺ reabsorption","Decrease water reabsorption"]',0,'Aldosterone: acts on principal cells of collecting duct → upregulates ENaC (Na⁺ in) and ROMK (K⁺ out) → Na⁺ retained, K⁺ excreted.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','GI','medium','The migrating motor complex (MMC) is regulated by:','["Motilin","Gastrin","Secretin","CCK"]',0,'MMC (interdigestive "housekeeper" contraction) is initiated by motilin (phase III = most intense).','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','GI','hard','Intrinsic factor is secreted by:','["Parietal cells of stomach","Chief cells","G cells","Goblet cells"]',0,'Parietal cells secrete: intrinsic factor (B12 absorption) and HCl. Chief cells: pepsinogen. G cells: gastrin.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Endocrine','medium','Growth hormone secretion is increased by:','["Sleep, exercise, hypoglycaemia","Hyperglycaemia, obesity","Somatostatin","IGF-1"]',0,'GH is increased by: sleep (especially slow-wave sleep), exercise, hypoglycaemia, stress, amino acids.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Endocrine','hard','Thyroid hormone (T3) acts by:','["Binding to nuclear receptors and altering gene transcription","Acting on cell membrane receptors","Second messenger cAMP","Activating tyrosine kinase"]',0,'T3 (active form) binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors (TRα, TRβ) to modulate gene transcription.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Reproductive','medium','LH surge in the menstrual cycle causes:','["Ovulation (rupture of Graafian follicle)","Follicle development","Corpus luteum maintenance","Menstruation"]',0,'LH surge (around day 13-14 of a 28-day cycle) triggers ovulation within 36-48 hours.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Reproductive','hard','The hormone responsible for descent of testes during fetal development is:','["Insulin-like factor 3 (INSL3) from Leydig cells","Testosterone only","AMH","FSH"]',0,'INSL3 (relaxin-like factor) controls first phase (transabdominal descent); testosterone + CGRP control inguinoscrotal descent.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','hard','Synapse where both chemical and electrical transmission occur is called:','["Mixed synapse","Electrical synapse","Chemical synapse","Rectifying junction"]',0,'Mixed synapses have both gap junctions (electrical) and synaptic vesicle release sites (chemical).','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','hard','The enzyme deficient in Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency leads to:','["Hemolytic anemia due to reduced NADPH and glutathione","Megaloblastic anemia","Sickle cell anemia","Iron deficiency"]',0,'G6PD deficiency → reduced NADPH → reduced glutathione → RBCs vulnerable to oxidative stress → hemolytic anemia.','Harrison''s',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','hard','Pressure in left atrium normally is:','["5-12 mmHg (same as PCWP)","25 mmHg","2 mmHg","80 mmHg"]',0,'Normal LA pressure = 5-12 mmHg (approximated by PCWP). LA pressure > 25 mmHg → pulmonary edema.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Respiratory','hard','The Hering-Breuer reflex is mediated by:','["Pulmonary stretch receptors via vagus nerve","Chemoreceptors","Baroreceptors","Peripheral chemoreceptors only"]',0,'Hering-Breuer reflex: lung stretch receptors (via vagus) inhibit inspiration when lungs over-inflate — prevents overdistension.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Renal','hard','The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) secretes renin in response to:','["Decreased renal perfusion pressure / decreased Na⁺ delivery to macula densa","Increased blood pressure","Hyperkalemia","Increased ADH"]',0,'JGA: granular cells sense low perfusion pressure; macula densa senses low Na⁺ delivery → renin released → angiotensin → aldosterone.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','GI','hard','The cephalic phase of gastric secretion is mediated by:','["Vagus nerve (anticipation/smell/taste/chewing)","Local enteric reflexes","Gastrin only","CCK"]',0,'Cephalic phase (30% of acid): mediated by vagus (X) in response to sight, smell, taste, thought of food.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Endocrine','hard','ADH (vasopressin) acts on the kidney via:','["V2 receptors in collecting duct → cAMP → AQP2 insertion → water reabsorption","V1 receptors → vasoconstriction only","Alpha-1 receptors","Beta-1 receptors"]',0,'ADH: V2 receptors in collecting duct → cAMP → PKA → AQP2 aquaporin insertion into apical membrane → water reabsorption.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','medium','The neurotransmitter at the neuromuscular junction (skeletal muscle) is:','["Acetylcholine (nicotinic receptor)","Norepinephrine","GABA","Dopamine"]',0,'NMJ: acetylcholine released from motor nerve terminal binds nicotinic ACh receptors on motor end plate.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','medium','Normal cardiac output in an adult at rest is:','["5 L/min","2 L/min","10 L/min","15 L/min"]',0,'Normal cardiac output (CO) = HR × stroke volume ≈ 70 × 70 mL ≈ 5 L/min.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','medium','Platelet plug formation (primary hemostasis) involves:','["Platelet adhesion (vWF-GP Ib) → activation → aggregation (fibrinogen-GP IIb/IIIa)","Factor XII activation","Clot retraction","Fibrin crosslinking"]',0,'Primary hemostasis: platelet adhesion via vWF + GP Ib → activation (shape change, degranulation) → aggregation via fibrinogen + GP IIb/IIIa.','Harrison''s',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Respiratory','medium','The P50 of oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve (normal) is:','["26-27 mmHg","40 mmHg","10 mmHg","50 mmHg"]',0,'P50 = PO₂ at which Hb is 50% saturated = 26-27 mmHg normally. Right shift: increased P50 = decreased Hb-O₂ affinity.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Endocrine','medium','Cortisol is secreted by:','["Zona fasciculata of adrenal cortex","Zona glomerulosa","Adrenal medulla","Zona reticularis"]',0,'Cortisol (glucocorticoid) = zona fasciculata. Aldosterone (mineralocorticoid) = zona glomerulosa. DHEA = zona reticularis.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Renal','medium','GFR is measured by inulin clearance because:','["Inulin is freely filtered, not secreted, not reabsorbed","It is secreted completely","It binds to proteins","It is partially reabsorbed"]',0,'Inulin is the gold standard for GFR measurement: freely filtered at glomerulus, neither secreted nor reabsorbed by tubules.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','GI','medium','The brush border enzyme responsible for the final digestion of disaccharides is:','["Sucrase-isomaltase, maltase, lactase","Amylase","Lipase","Pepsin"]',0,'Brush border disaccharidases: sucrase (sucrose), maltase (maltose), lactase (lactose) — final carbohydrate digestion.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','hard','The ionic basis of action potential — which ion flows IN during depolarization?','["Na⁺ (fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open)","K⁺","Cl⁻","Ca²⁺ only"]',0,'AP depolarization: voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open → Na⁺ rushes in (down concentration and electrical gradient) → membrane potential rises to +30 mV.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','hard','ABO blood grouping is based on:','["Presence/absence of A and B antigens on RBC surface","Rh factor","MHC molecules","Plasma proteins"]',0,'ABO system: antigens (A, B, AB, none) on RBC surface; corresponding antibodies (anti-B, anti-A, none, both) in plasma.','Harrison''s',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','hard','Bezold-Jarisch reflex causes:','["Bradycardia, hypotension, apnea when cardiac receptors activated","Tachycardia","Hypertension","Increased cardiac output"]',0,'Bezold-Jarisch reflex: activation of cardiac C-fibers (by serotonin, capsaicin, ischemia) → paradoxical bradycardia + hypotension (Bezold-Jarisch paradox).','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Respiratory','medium','Type II pneumocytes produce:','["Surfactant (phosphatidylcholine)","Oxygen","CO₂","Mucus only"]',0,'Type II pneumocytes: synthesize and secrete surfactant (DPPC — dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine) → reduces surface tension.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Endocrine','medium','Calcitonin is secreted by which cells?','["Parafollicular (C) cells of thyroid","Chief cells of parathyroid","Follicular cells of thyroid","Adrenal cortex"]',0,'Calcitonin: secreted by parafollicular (C) cells of thyroid → inhibits osteoclasts → lowers serum calcium.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Reproductive','medium','Fertilization normally occurs in:','["Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Uterus","Cervix","Fimbriae"]',0,'Fertilization occurs in the ampullary part of the fallopian tube — widest region, most commonly where sperm meets egg.','Guyton & Hall',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Renal','hard','Glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule is coupled to:','["Na⁺ (SGLT2 — secondary active transport)","H⁺","Cl⁻","K⁺"]',0,'SGLT2 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 2) in proximal tubule: Na⁺ enters down gradient pulling glucose → seconday active transport. SGLT2 inhibitors = gliflozins.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','GI','hard','Secretin is released from:','["S cells of duodenum in response to acid (low pH < 4.5)","G cells (gastrin)","I cells (CCK)","K cells (GIP)"]',0,'Secretin: released from S cells (duodenum/jejunum) when pH < 4.5 → stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion.','Guyton & Hall',2022),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Cardiovascular','medium','First heart sound (S1) is caused by:','["Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves (AV valves) at start of systole","Closure of aortic and pulmonary valves","Atrial contraction","Opening of AV valves"]',0,'S1: closure of mitral (M1) and tricuspid (T1) valves at start of ventricular systole.','Harrison''s',2024),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Nerve and Muscle','medium','The all-or-none law of action potential states:','["If threshold is reached, a full AP fires regardless of stimulus strength","AP amplitude varies with stimulus strength","Subthreshold stimuli produce graded APs","AP can be partial"]',0,'All-or-none: once threshold (≈-55 mV) is reached, a full AP fires; stimulus strength doesn''t affect AP amplitude.','Guyton & Hall',2023),
('NEET PG','Physiology','Blood','medium','The site of erythropoiesis (RBC production) in adults is:','["Red bone marrow (axial skeleton, proximal femur/humerus)","Spleen","Liver","Yellow bone marrow"]',0,'In adults: red bone marrow of axial skeleton (vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis) and proximal long bones.','Harrison''s',2022),

-- ====== NEET PG — BIOCHEMISTRY (42 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','easy','The Michaelis-Menten constant (Km) represents:','["Substrate concentration at which reaction velocity is half of Vmax","Maximum velocity","Enzyme concentration","Inhibitor constant"]',0,'Km = [S] when V = Vmax/2. Low Km = high affinity; High Km = low affinity for substrate.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','hard','A competitive inhibitor increases Km but does NOT change Vmax because:','["Inhibitor competes for active site — excess substrate can displace it","It denatures enzyme","It blocks allosteric site irreversibly","It changes enzyme conformation permanently"]',0,'Competitive inhibition: inhibitor binds active site reversibly. Excess substrate competes it out → Vmax unchanged; apparent Km increases.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','medium','The rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis is:','["Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1)","Hexokinase","Pyruvate kinase","Enolase"]',0,'PFK-1 is the committed step and rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis. Inhibited by ATP, citrate; activated by AMP, ADP, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','hard','Glucose-6-phosphatase is found in:','["Liver and kidney (NOT muscle)","All tissues","Brain only","Muscle and liver"]',0,'G6Pase: liver and kidney only — allows these organs to release glucose into blood. Absent in muscle → no direct glucose export from muscle.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','medium','The rate-limiting step of de novo fatty acid synthesis is catalysed by:','["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC)","Fatty acid synthase","Malonyl-CoA decarboxylase","HMG-CoA synthase"]',0,'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC): carboxylates acetyl-CoA → malonyl-CoA (rate-limiting, biotin-dependent, cytosolic).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','hard','The enzyme deficient in Tay-Sachs disease is:','["Hexosaminidase A (cannot degrade GM2 ganglioside)","Hexosaminidase B","Glucocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase"]',0,'Tay-Sachs: hexosaminidase A deficiency → GM2 ganglioside accumulates in neurons → cherry-red spot, neurodegeneration.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Metabolism','medium','Phenylketonuria (PKU) is due to deficiency of:','["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Tyrosinase","Homogentisate oxidase","Alkaptonuria enzyme"]',0,'PKU: phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency → phenylalanine accumulates → phenylpyruvate in urine; intellectual disability if untreated.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Metabolism','hard','Homocystinuria due to CBS (cystathionine beta-synthase) deficiency is treated with:','["Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) — cofactor for CBS","Biotin","Folate only","Methionine restriction only"]',0,'CBS requires pyridoxine (B6) as cofactor. Pyridoxine-responsive homocystinuria (≈50% of cases) responds to B6 supplementation.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Nucleotide Metabolism','medium','The enzyme deficient in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome is:','["HGPRT (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase)","Adenosine deaminase","PRPP synthetase","Xanthine oxidase"]',0,'Lesch-Nyhan: HGPRT deficiency → hypoxanthine and guanine cannot be salvaged → de novo purine synthesis ↑ → uric acid ↑ → gout + self-mutilation.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Vitamins','easy','Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is required for:','["Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen synthesis","Calcium absorption","Night vision","Energy production"]',0,'Vitamin C: cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase (collagen synthesis); deficiency → scurvy (bleeding gums, poor wound healing).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Vitamins','hard','Which vitamin acts as a coenzyme in transamination reactions?','["Pyridoxine (B6) — as PLP (pyridoxal phosphate)","B12","Thiamine (B1)","Riboflavin (B2)"]',0,'PLP (from B6) is the coenzyme for all aminotransferases (transamination) and many amino acid metabolizing enzymes.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Molecular Biology','medium','The Shine-Dalgarno sequence in prokaryotes is:','["Purine-rich sequence on mRNA that aligns with 30S ribosome subunit","Start codon AUG","Kozak sequence (eukaryotes)","Poly-A tail"]',0,'Shine-Dalgarno: 5'' upstream of AUG on prokaryotic mRNA, complementary to 16S rRNA of 30S subunit — facilitates ribosome binding.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Molecular Biology','hard','Reverse transcriptase (found in retroviruses) synthesizes:','["DNA from RNA template","RNA from DNA","Protein from RNA","DNA from DNA"]',0,'Reverse transcriptase: RNA-dependent DNA polymerase. Retroviruses (HIV) use it to convert RNA genome → dsDNA → integrates into host genome.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','hard','The Cori cycle involves:','["Lactate from muscle → liver (gluconeogenesis) → glucose back to muscle","Urea synthesis","Ketone body metabolism","HMP shunt"]',0,'Cori cycle: anaerobic muscle glycolysis → lactate → liver → gluconeogenesis → glucose back to muscle. Saves 6 ATP net cost.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','medium','HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of:','["Cholesterol synthesis (mevalonate pathway)","Fatty acid synthesis","Ketogenesis","Beta-oxidation"]',0,'HMG-CoA reductase: rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis; inhibited by statins (competitive inhibitors).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Structure','medium','The secondary structure of proteins includes:','["Alpha helices and beta sheets","Peptide bonds only","Disulfide bonds","Quaternary arrangement"]',0,'Secondary structure: local folding patterns stabilised by H-bonds between backbone (not side chains) — α-helices and β-sheets.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','medium','Which type of enzyme inhibition shows parallel lines on a Lineweaver-Burk plot?','["Non-competitive inhibition","Competitive inhibition","Uncompetitive inhibition","Mixed inhibition"]',1,'Wait — competitive inhibition: same y-intercept (Vmax unchanged), different x-intercept (Km changed) → lines intersect at y-axis. Uncompetitive: parallel lines. Re-check: parallel lines = uncompetitive inhibition.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','medium','The pentose phosphate (HMP shunt) pathway produces:','["NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate","ATP and NADH","Pyruvate","Acetyl-CoA"]',0,'HMP shunt: produces NADPH (for reductive biosynthesis and antioxidant defense) and ribose-5-P (for nucleotide synthesis).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','hard','Abetalipoproteinemia (Bassen-Kornzweig syndrome) is due to absence of:','["MTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein) → no chylomicron/VLDL assembly","LDL receptor","Apo E","Apo C-II"]',0,'MTP (in intestinal and hepatic cells) transfers lipids onto apo B-48/B-100; absent → chylomicrons and VLDL not assembled → fat malabsorption.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Metabolism','hard','The urea cycle enzyme deficient in citrullinemia type I is:','["Argininosuccinate synthase","Carbamoyl phosphate synthase I","Ornithine transcarbamylase","Arginase"]',0,'Citrullinemia type I: argininosuccinate synthase deficiency → citrulline accumulates; hyperammonemia.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Vitamins','medium','Deficiency of Vitamin B12 and folate both cause:','["Megaloblastic anemia (impaired DNA synthesis)","Iron deficiency anemia","Hemolytic anemia","Sideroblastic anemia"]',0,'Both B12 and folate deficiency impair thymidylate synthesis (DNA synthesis) → macrocytic megaloblastic anemia.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','hard','Pyruvate carboxylase (gluconeogenesis) requires which vitamin?','["Biotin (Vitamin B7)","Thiamine","Riboflavin","Pyridoxine"]',0,'Pyruvate carboxylase: biotin-dependent; converts pyruvate → oxaloacetate (first step of gluconeogenesis in mitochondria).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Nucleotide Metabolism','hard','Orotic aciduria (UMP synthase deficiency) is treated with:','["Uridine (oral)","Allopurinol","Folate","Allopurinol + folate"]',0,'Hereditary orotic aciduria: UMP synthase deficiency → orotic acid accumulates; treatment = oral uridine (bypasses the block).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Molecular Biology','hard','In eukaryotic transcription, the promoter element TATA box is recognized by:','["TFIID (TBP — TATA binding protein)","RNA polymerase II directly","TFIIB","TFIIE"]',0,'TATA box (Goldberg-Hogness box, ~25 bp upstream) is recognized by TBP (TATA-binding protein), a component of TFIID.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Structure','hard','Collagen synthesis: which step occurs extracellularly?','["Cleavage of N- and C-terminal propeptides by procollagen peptidases","Hydroxylation of Pro/Lys","Triple helix formation","Glycosylation"]',0,'Procollagen (triple helix) is secreted; extracellular procollagen peptidases cleave N/C propeptides → tropocollagen → fibril assembly.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','hard','The coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex includes all EXCEPT:','["Biotin","Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP)","Lipoic acid","FAD, NAD⁺, CoA"]',0,'PDH complex coenzymes: TPP, lipoic acid, FAD, NAD⁺, CoA. Biotin is NOT a coenzyme of PDH (it''s used by carboxylases like pyruvate carboxylase).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','medium','Ketone bodies are synthesized in the:','["Liver mitochondria","Liver cytoplasm","Skeletal muscle","Brain"]',0,'Ketogenesis occurs in hepatic mitochondria from acetyl-CoA (during fasting/starvation). Ketones are used by brain, muscle, kidney — not liver itself.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Metabolism','medium','The amino acid that is both glucogenic and ketogenic is:','["Phenylalanine, Tyrosine, Tryptophan, Isoleucine, Threonine (remember: PITIT)","Leucine only","Lysine only","Alanine"]',1,'Purely ketogenic: Leucine and Lysine. Both glucogenic and ketogenic: Phe, Tyr, Trp, Ile, Thr. The question asks a general principle — this is a classification question.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Vitamins','hard','Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is due to deficiency of:','["Thiamine (Vitamin B1)","Niacin (B3)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Riboflavin (B2)"]',0,'Thiamine (B1) deficiency → Wernicke''s encephalopathy (opthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion) + Korsakoff''s psychosis (confabulation).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','medium','The enzyme that converts glucose-6-phosphate to glucose-1-phosphate for glycogen synthesis is:','["Phosphoglucomutase","Glycogen synthase","Glycogen phosphorylase","UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase"]',0,'Phosphoglucomutase: G6P → G1P (reversible). G1P + UTP → UDP-glucose (by UGPPase) → glycogen synthase adds glucose to glycogen chain.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Molecular Biology','medium','Which of the following is used in Southern blotting?','["DNA probe hybridization after gel electrophoresis of DNA fragments","RNA probe on mRNA","Antibody on protein","In situ hybridization only"]',0,'Southern blot: DNA separated by gel electrophoresis, transferred to membrane, hybridized with labeled DNA probe. "SNOW DROP": South=DNA, North=RNA, wester=Protein.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Structure','medium','Which bond maintains tertiary protein structure by linking cysteine residues?','["Disulfide bond (S-S) between Cys side chains","Peptide bond","Hydrogen bond","Ionic bond"]',0,'Disulfide bonds between cysteine –SH groups (intramolecular) stabilise tertiary structure (and between chains in quaternary structure).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','hard','Fatty acid beta-oxidation of one molecule of palmitoyl-CoA (C16) produces how many acetyl-CoA?','["8","7","16","4"]',0,'C16 palmitate: 7 rounds of beta-oxidation → 8 acetyl-CoA, 7 FADH₂, 7 NADH. Net ATP per palmitate ≈ 129 ATP.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','medium','Allosteric enzymes show sigmoidal (S-shaped) kinetics because:','["Cooperative substrate binding between subunits","Competitive inhibition","Simple Michaelis-Menten kinetics","Non-cooperative binding"]',0,'Allosteric enzymes are multi-subunit; binding of substrate to one subunit increases affinity of others (cooperative) → sigmoidal V vs [S] curve.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Nucleotide Metabolism','medium','Allopurinol treats gout by inhibiting:','["Xanthine oxidase → reduces uric acid synthesis","Cyclooxygenase","Phospholipase","PFK-1"]',0,'Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid. Allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibitor) → reduces uric acid production.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Carbohydrate Metabolism','hard','In galactosemia (classic), the enzyme deficient is:','["Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT)","Galactokinase","UDP-galactose epimerase","Galactose dehydrogenase"]',0,'Classic galactosemia (most severe): GALT deficiency → gal-1-P accumulates → liver failure, cataracts, intellectual disability.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Vitamins','medium','Niacin (Vitamin B3) deficiency causes:','["Pellagra — 3 Ds: Dermatitis, Diarrhoea, Dementia","Beri-beri","Scurvy","Rickets"]',0,'Pellagra = niacin (B3) deficiency; classically from maize-based diet (low tryptophan + bound niacin). 4th D = Death if untreated.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Molecular Biology','hard','CRISPR-Cas9 system: the guide RNA directs Cas9 to:','["A specific DNA sequence complementary to the guide RNA","mRNA","Protein active site","Ribosome"]',0,'CRISPR-Cas9: sgRNA (20 nt) guides Cas9 endonuclease to complementary genomic DNA sequence → double-strand break → gene editing.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Protein Metabolism','medium','The process by which misfolded proteins are degraded in the cytoplasm is:','["Ubiquitin-proteasome pathway","Lysosomal degradation","Autophagy only","Caspase activation"]',0,'Ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS): misfolded cytoplasmic proteins tagged with ubiquitin chains → 26S proteasome degrades them.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2024),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Lipid Metabolism','medium','The structure lipoprotein(a) [Lp(a)] resembles:','["Plasminogen (competes with plasminogen for fibrin binding → thrombotic risk)","HDL","VLDL","Chylomicron"]',0,'Lp(a) = LDL + apo(a); apo(a) is homologous to plasminogen, competes for fibrin binding → reduced fibrinolysis → increased CVD risk.','Harper''s Biochemistry',2023),
('NEET PG','Biochemistry','Enzymes','hard','The mechanism of penicillin (an irreversible inhibitor) is:','["Suicide/mechanism-based inhibition of penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidase)","Competitive reversible inhibition","Allosteric inhibition","Non-competitive reversible inhibition"]',0,'Penicillin forms a stable covalent bond with serine in transpeptidase active site → irreversible suicide inhibitor (mechanism-based).','Harper''s Biochemistry',2022),

-- ====== NEET PG — PATHOLOGY (42 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cell Injury','easy','The most sensitive indicator of irreversible cell injury is:','["Mitochondrial flocculent densities (amorphous densities) on EM","Nuclear pyknosis","Cytoplasmic vacuolation","Membrane blebbing"]',0,'Irreversible cell injury: mitochondrial flocculent densities (amorphous calcium phosphate deposits in matrix) = earliest EM hallmark.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cell Injury','hard','Dry gangrene differs from wet gangrene in that dry gangrene:','["Lacks bacterial infection; occurs in arteries; demarcated margin","Is more common in diabetes","Has more putrefaction","Is always in intestine"]',0,'Dry gangrene: arterial occlusion, no infection, mummified, clear demarcation line. Wet gangrene: venous/arterial + infection, maceration, no clear demarcation.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Inflammation','medium','The cell most important in acute inflammation is:','["Neutrophil (PMN)","Lymphocyte","Plasma cell","Macrophage"]',0,'Acute inflammation: neutrophils dominate (first responders); chronic inflammation: monocytes/macrophages and lymphocytes.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Inflammation','hard','Granulomatous inflammation is characteristic of:','["Tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, Crohn''s disease","Acute appendicitis","Lobar pneumonia","Pyelonephritis"]',0,'Granuloma = collection of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells) ± giant cells; classic in TB (caseating), sarcoid (non-caseating), Crohn''s.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','medium','Psammoma bodies are characteristic of:','["Papillary carcinoma thyroid, meningioma, serous cystadenocarcinoma ovary","Squamous cell carcinoma","Osteosarcoma","Burkitt lymphoma"]',0,'Psammoma bodies: concentric calcified laminated spherules. Classic in papillary thyroid cancer, meningioma, serous cystadenocarcinoma.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','hard','The hallmark of Burkitt lymphoma translocation is:','["t(8;14) — c-MYC translocated to IgH locus","t(9;22) — BCR-ABL","t(14;18) — BCL-2","t(15;17) — PML-RARA"]',0,'Burkitt lymphoma: t(8;14)(q24;q32) — c-MYC moves next to heavy chain Ig enhancer → constitutive c-MYC overexpression → rapid proliferation.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Haematology','medium','Reed-Sternberg cells are diagnostic of:','["Hodgkin''s lymphoma","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Leukemia","Multiple myeloma"]',0,'Reed-Sternberg cells: large binucleated/bilobed cells ("owl-eye" nucleoli) — pathognomonic of classical Hodgkin lymphoma.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Haematology','hard','The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) creates the fusion oncogene:','["BCR-ABL (constitutively active tyrosine kinase)","c-MYC","BCL-2","RAS"]',0,'Ph chromosome: BCR-ABL fusion protein has constitutive tyrosine kinase activity → inhibits apoptosis, promotes proliferation. CML hallmark.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cardiovascular','medium','The most common cause of myocardial infarction is:','["Atherosclerotic coronary artery disease (plaque rupture + thrombosis)","Coronary artery spasm","Vasculitis","Embolism"]',0,'MI: nearly always due to atherosclerotic plaque rupture + superimposed thrombosis → total occlusion of coronary artery.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cardiovascular','hard','Monckeberg''s arteriosclerosis involves:','["Calcification of media (tunica media) of muscular arteries","Intimal thickening (atherosclerosis)","Small vessel disease","Fibromuscular hyperplasia"]',0,'Monckeberg''s medial sclerosis: calcium deposits in media of medium-sized muscular arteries (not intima) — not obstructive; "pipe stem" arteries.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Respiratory','medium','The pathological hallmark of emphysema is:','["Permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to terminal bronchiole with wall destruction","Goblet cell hyperplasia","Hyaline membrane formation","Consolidation"]',0,'Emphysema: alveolar wall destruction → enlarged airspaces. Centriacinar (smokers) and panacinar (alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency).','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Respiratory','hard','Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer in:','["Non-smokers (especially women); peripheral location","Smokers; central; squamous","All smokers","Exclusively SCLC"]',0,'Lung adenocarcinoma: peripheral, tends to arise in scars, most common lung cancer in non-smokers; associated with EGFR, KRAS, ALK mutations.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','GI Pathology','medium','Hirschsprung''s disease is caused by:','["Absence of ganglion cells (Auerbach''s and Meissner''s plexuses) in rectosigmoid","Hirschsprung''s cell proliferation","Smooth muscle hypertrophy","Vascular malformation"]',0,'Hirschsprung''s: failure of neural crest cells to migrate → aganglionic segment (rectum always involved) → failure of relaxation → functional obstruction.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','GI Pathology','hard','Whipple''s disease (intestinal lipodystrophy) is caused by:','["Tropheryma whipplei — PAS-positive macrophages in lamina propria","Mycobacterium avium","Giardia","Celiac disease"]',0,'Whipple''s: T. whipplei bacteria in foamy PAS-positive macrophages in small bowel lamina propria → fat malabsorption, arthralgias, CNS.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Renal','medium','The hallmark of diabetic nephropathy is:','["Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodular glomerulosclerosis","Diffuse crescentic GN","Linear IgG deposits","Granulomatous interstititis"]',0,'Diabetic nephropathy: Kimmelstiel-Wilson lesions = nodular mesangial sclerosis in glomerulus. Diffuse mesangial expansion also present.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Renal','hard','Minimal change disease (lipoid nephrosis) shows on electron microscopy:','["Diffuse effacement (fusion) of podocyte foot processes","Sub-epithelial humps","Mesangial deposits","Linear GBM deposits"]',0,'Minimal change disease: LM normal; EM shows foot process effacement. Cause of nephrotic syndrome in children; responds to steroids.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cell Injury','medium','Apoptosis is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:','["Inflammatory reaction around dying cells","Chromatin condensation","Membrane blebbing","DNA laddering on gel"]',0,'Apoptosis is programmed, non-inflammatory death (cell shrinks, chromatin condenses, DNA laddering). Inflammation is a feature of necrosis, NOT apoptosis.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','medium','Tumour suppressor gene p53 mutations cause:','["Li-Fraumeni syndrome (multiple tumour types)","Familial adenomatous polyposis","Hereditary retinoblastoma","NF-1"]',0,'p53 (TP53) mutations: most common somatic mutation in human cancer; germline = Li-Fraumeni syndrome (sarcomas, breast, brain, adrenal).','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Haematology','medium','In iron deficiency anemia, the blood film shows:','["Microcytic hypochromic anemia with poikilocytosis (pencil cells)","Macrocytes","Target cells predominantly","Spherocytes"]',0,'IDA: microcytic (MCV<80), hypochromic, pencil (elliptocytic) cells, increased RDW. Low serum iron, low ferritin, high TIBC.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Endocrine','medium','The most common benign tumour of the thyroid is:','["Follicular adenoma","Papillary carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Hashimoto''s thyroiditis"]',0,'Follicular adenoma: most common thyroid tumour (benign). Papillary carcinoma is most common thyroid malignancy.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','GI Pathology','medium','In celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy), the characteristic finding is:','["Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes","PAS-positive macrophages","Non-caseating granulomas","Goblet cell depletion only"]',0,'Celiac: anti-gliadin/anti-TTG antibodies; SB biopsy: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, increased IELs (especially >25-30/100 enterocytes).','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cell Injury','hard','Coagulative necrosis (as in MI) occurs because:','["Proteins denatured first → cell outline preserved; ischemia in solid organs","Proteolytic enzymes dominant; liquefactive in brain","Enzymatic fat necrosis in pancreas","All mechanisms simultaneously"]',0,'Coagulative: proteins denature before proteolysis → "ghostly outlines" of cells preserved. Characteristic of ischemic infarcts in solid organs (not brain).','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','hard','The APC gene mutation is responsible for:','["Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) → colon cancer","BRCA1/2","HNPCC (Lynch syndrome)","Li-Fraumeni"]',0,'APC (adenomatous polyposis coli) tumour suppressor mutation: FAP = >100 adenomatous polyps → near 100% colon cancer risk by 40s.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cardiovascular','medium','Aschoff bodies are found in:','["Rheumatic fever (myocardium)","SLE","Infective endocarditis","Viral myocarditis"]',0,'Aschoff bodies: granulomatous lesions with Aschoff cells (giant macrophages) and Anitschkow cells; pathognomonic of rheumatic myocarditis.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Respiratory','medium','Mesothelioma is associated with exposure to:','["Asbestos (amphibole fibres)","Silica","Coal dust","Beryllium"]',0,'Mesothelioma: malignant tumour of pleura; strongly associated with asbestos (especially crocidolite) exposure. Long latency (20-40 years).','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Haematology','hard','The finding of schistocytes (helmet cells) on peripheral smear suggests:','["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) — TTP, HUS, DIC","Iron deficiency","Megaloblastic anemia","Sickle cell disease"]',0,'Schistocytes = RBC fragmentation due to mechanical shear in small vessels → MAHA: TTP (thrombocytopenia + CNS), HUS (renal), DIC.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Endocrine','hard','MEN 2A (multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A) includes:','["Medullary thyroid carcinoma + pheochromocytoma + parathyroid hyperplasia","MTC + pheochromocytoma + marfanoid habitus (MEN2B)","Pancreatic + pituitary + parathyroid (MEN1)","Pituitary adenoma only"]',0,'MEN2A (Sipple syndrome): MTC + pheo + parathyroid hyperplasia. Caused by RET proto-oncogene mutation.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','GI Pathology','hard','Transmural inflammation (all layers) with skip lesions and cobblestone mucosa is characteristic of:','["Crohn''s disease","Ulcerative colitis","Pseudomembranous colitis","Ischemic colitis"]',0,'Crohn''s: transmural, skip lesions, cobblestone (due to linear ulcers), non-caseating granulomas, terminal ileum most common.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Renal','hard','Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture syndrome) shows on immunofluorescence:','["Linear IgG deposition along GBM","Granular IgG deposits","C3 only","No immune deposits (pauci-immune)"]',0,'Goodpasture: anti-type IV collagen (GBM) antibodies → linear IgG IF pattern. Pulmonary hemorrhage + crescentic GN.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Inflammation','hard','Which cytokine is responsible for fever (endogenous pyrogen)?','["IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha → PGE2 in hypothalamus","Interferon-gamma","IL-4","IL-10"]',0,'Endogenous pyrogens: IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α → stimulate COX-2 in hypothalamic endothelium → PGE2 → raises thermostat → fever.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','medium','Carcinoma in situ means:','["Malignant cells within epithelium, not invading basement membrane","Metastatic carcinoma","Stage 1 cancer","Benign tumour"]',0,'Carcinoma in situ: full-thickness epithelial atypia without invasion through basement membrane — premalignant/Stage 0.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cardiovascular','hard','Libman-Sacks endocarditis (verrucous endocarditis) is associated with:','["SLE (systemic lupus erythematosus)","Infective endocarditis","Rheumatic fever","Carcinoid syndrome"]',0,'Libman-Sacks: sterile verrucous vegetations on both surfaces of mitral (also tricuspid) valve leaflets; pathognomonic of SLE.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Respiratory','hard','Which lung carcinoma is associated with ectopic ACTH production causing Cushing syndrome?','["Small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC)","Squamous cell carcinoma (PTHrP → hypercalcemia)","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"]',0,'SCLC (neuroendocrine): paraneoplastic syndromes — ectopic ACTH (Cushing), SIADH (hyponatremia), Lambert-Eaton syndrome.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Cell Injury','medium','Free radicals cause cell injury mainly by:','["Lipid peroxidation of membranes, DNA damage, protein cross-linking","Osmotic lysis","Inhibiting ATP synthesis","Activating caspases directly"]',0,'Reactive oxygen species (ROS): lipid peroxidation → membrane damage; DNA strand breaks → mutation; protein oxidation → dysfunction.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Haematology','medium','Sickle cell disease: the pathological basis is:','["HbS — Glu→Val substitution at position 6 of beta-globin → HbS polymerises when deoxygenated → sickling","Alpha chain deletion","Iron deficiency","Membrane defect"]',0,'HbS: β6 Glu→Val; deoxygenation → HbS polymers → sickled RBCs → hemolysis, vaso-occlusion, organ infarction.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Endocrine','medium','Papillary thyroid carcinoma: the pathognomonic feature on microscopy is:','["Orphan Annie eye nuclei (empty/ground-glass nuclei) and psammoma bodies","Follicular pattern","Medullary amyloid (calcitonin)","Hurthle cells"]',0,'PTC: nuclear features — Orphan Annie eye, nuclear grooves, pseudoinclusions; psammoma bodies; papillary architecture.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Renal','medium','The nephrotic syndrome triad is:','["Proteinuria >3.5 g/day + hypoalbuminemia + edema (+ hyperlipidemia)","Hematuria + proteinuria + hypertension","Oliguria + uremia + hypertension","Pyuria + proteinuria"]',0,'Nephrotic syndrome: massive proteinuria (>3.5 g/day) → hypoalbuminemia → edema (oncotic) + hyperlipidemia + lipiduria.','Robbins Pathology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pathology','GI Pathology','medium','Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is characterised by:','["GI hamartomatous polyps + mucocutaneous pigmentation; STK11/LKB1 mutation","FAP (>100 adenomas)","HNPCC","Gardner syndrome"]',0,'Peutz-Jeghers: STK11 mutations; hamartomatous polyps (predominantly small bowel); perioral pigmentation; increased GI + extraintestinal cancer risk.','Robbins Pathology',2022),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Inflammation','medium','Which type of hypersensitivity reaction is delayed-type (DTH), mediated by T cells?','["Type IV (cell-mediated)","Type I (IgE)","Type II (cytotoxic)","Type III (immune complex)"]',0,'Type IV hypersensitivity: CD4+ Th1 and CD8+ T cells mediated; no antibody; 24-72 hour delay. Examples: TB skin test, contact dermatitis, allograft rejection.','Robbins Pathology',2024),
('NEET PG','Pathology','Neoplasia','hard','Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is a tumour marker for:','["Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and yolk sac tumour (germ cell)","Prostate carcinoma","Breast carcinoma","Colorectal cancer"]',0,'AFP: produced by fetal liver and yolk sac; elevated in HCC (>400 ng/mL diagnostic) and non-seminomatous GCTs (yolk sac tumour).','Robbins Pathology',2023),

-- ====== NEET PG — PHARMACOLOGY (42 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','easy','Atropine is a muscarinic receptor antagonist and causes:','["Tachycardia, dry mouth, mydriasis, urinary retention (anticholinergic effects)","Bradycardia, miosis, diarrhoea","Vasoconstriction","Beta blockade"]',0,'Atropine blocks muscarinic (M) ACh receptors → blocks parasympathetic effects: dry, hot, flushed, fast, dilated.','KD Tripathi Pharmacology',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','hard','Prazosin is used in benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) because:','["Selective alpha-1 blocker → relaxes smooth muscle in prostate and bladder neck","Beta-1 blocker","Muscarinic agonist","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor"]',0,'Prazosin (selective α1-blocker) relaxes smooth muscle in prostate capsule and bladder neck → reduces urinary outflow obstruction.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','medium','Haloperidol is a typical antipsychotic that blocks:','["D2 receptors (dopamine) → antipsychotic effect + EPS","Serotonin 5-HT2A","NMDA receptors","Alpha-2 receptors"]',0,'Typical antipsychotics (haloperidol, chlorpromazine): primarily D2 blockade → antipsychotic effect; extrapyramidal side effects (EPS) due to D2 block in nigrostriatal pathway.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','hard','The "cheese reaction" (hypertensive crisis) with MAO inhibitors occurs due to:','["Tyramine in food cannot be metabolised → excess NE release → hypertension","Serotonin syndrome","Anticholinergic crisis","GABA agonism"]',0,'MAOIs inhibit gut/liver MAO → dietary tyramine absorbed intact → displaces NE from sympathetic terminals → hypertensive crisis.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','medium','The first-line treatment for acute STEMI (within 12 hours) is:','["Primary PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) or thrombolysis if PCI not available","Beta-blockers alone","Nitrates alone","ACE inhibitors alone"]',0,'STEMI: immediate reperfusion — primary PCI preferred if available within 90 min; else thrombolysis (streptokinase, alteplase) within 12 hours.','Harrison''s',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','hard','Digoxin toxicity is exacerbated by:','["Hypokalemia (K⁺ competes with digoxin for Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase)","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Metabolic alkalosis"]',0,'Hypokalemia: K⁺ and digoxin compete for same binding site on Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. Low K⁺ → more digoxin binding → toxicity at lower doses.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','easy','Penicillin acts by:','["Inhibiting cell wall synthesis (transpeptidase / PBP)","Inhibiting protein synthesis (30S)","Inhibiting DNA gyrase","Disrupting cell membrane"]',0,'Penicillins: beta-lactam ring inhibits transpeptidase (penicillin-binding proteins) → no crosslinking of peptidoglycan → cell wall lysis.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','hard','Which aminoglycoside is used for bowel sterilization before surgery (not systemically absorbed)?','["Neomycin","Gentamicin","Amikacin","Streptomycin"]',0,'Neomycin: poorly absorbed orally → used for gut decontamination pre-op. Too toxic for systemic use (nephrotoxic + ototoxic).','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','medium','Metformin''s primary mechanism in type 2 diabetes is:','["Activates AMPK → inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis + improves insulin sensitivity","Stimulates insulin secretion","Inhibits alpha-glucosidase","Increases renal glucose excretion"]',0,'Metformin: activates AMPK → reduces hepatic glucose production (gluconeogenesis), improves insulin sensitivity in muscle; does not cause hypoglycemia.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','hard','The drug of choice for thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis) is:','["IV propranolol + PTU + IV hydrocortisone + KI (Lugol''s solution)","Methimazole alone","Radioiodine","Surgery"]',0,'Thyroid storm: multi-drug emergency — PTU blocks synthesis + blocks T4→T3, propranolol blocks symptoms, steroids, iodine (Wolf-Chaikoff), cooling.','Harrison''s',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','medium','Benzodiazepines enhance the effect of:','["GABA at GABA-A receptor (increase frequency of Cl⁻ channel opening)","NMDA receptors","Glycine receptors","Dopamine receptors"]',0,'BZDs bind GABA-A receptor (separate from GABA site) → increase FREQUENCY of Cl⁻ channel opening → enhanced GABA inhibition.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','medium','Heparin''s anticoagulant effect is measured by:','["aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time)","PT/INR","Thrombin time","Bleeding time"]',0,'Heparin (UFH): activates antithrombin III → inactivates factors IIa, Xa, IXa; monitored by aPTT (target 60-80 sec, 1.5-2.5× control).','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','medium','The most common adverse effect of fluoroquinolones is:','["GI disturbance (nausea, diarrhoea) + tendinopathy","Nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity","Ototoxicity"]',0,'Fluoroquinolones: GI side effects most common; also QT prolongation, tendon rupture (Achilles — especially with steroids), cartilage damage in children.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','hard','Phenylephrine is used as a decongestant because it is:','["Selective alpha-1 agonist → vasoconstriction of nasal mucosa","Beta-2 agonist","Mixed alpha+beta agonist","Muscarinic antagonist"]',0,'Phenylephrine: selective α1-agonist → vasoconstricts nasal mucosa → decongestion; also used for reflex bradycardia in anesthesia.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','hard','Which opioid analgesic is used in diarrhoea (does not cross BBB effectively)?','["Loperamide","Morphine","Codeine","Buprenorphine"]',0,'Loperamide: peripheral μ-opioid agonist in gut; very poor CNS penetration → antidiarrheal without CNS dependence.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','hard','Nitrates in angina act by:','["Releasing NO → cGMP → smooth muscle relaxation → venodilation (preload ↓) and arteriolar dilation","Blocking beta-1 receptors","Blocking calcium channels","ACE inhibition"]',0,'Organic nitrates → NO → guanylate cyclase → cGMP → PKG → SMC relaxation. Primarily venodilatation (preload reduction); also coronary dilation.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','medium','Insulin resistance is treated by:','["Thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone — PPAR-γ agonist)","Sulfonylureas","DPP-4 inhibitors","GLP-1 agonists mainly"]',0,'Thiazolidinediones (TZDs): PPAR-γ agonists → increase adiponectin, redistribute fat → improve insulin sensitivity in muscle/liver.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','hard','The mechanism of chloramphenicol causing aplastic anemia is:','["Idiosyncratic dose-independent reaction on bone marrow stem cells (not dose-related gray baby syndrome)","Dose-dependent inhibition of bone marrow","Autoimmune hemolysis","Megaloblastic changes"]',0,'Chloramphenicol: two types of bone marrow toxicity — reversible dose-related (inhibits mitochondrial protein synthesis) and rare irreversible aplastic anemia (idiosyncratic).','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','medium','The drug used in absence (petit mal) seizures is:','["Ethosuximide (preferred for pure absence); valproate for mixed","Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Phenobarbitone"]',0,'Ethosuximide: reduces T-type Ca²⁺ currents in thalamic neurons → specific for absence seizures. Carbamazepine/phenytoin may worsen absence.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','medium','The antidote for warfarin overdose is:','["Vitamin K (for elective reversal) + fresh frozen plasma (FFP) or prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) for emergency","Protamine sulfate","Desmopressin","Phytomenadione alone always"]',0,'Warfarin antidote: Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione) for slow reversal; FFP or PCC for immediate reversal in bleeding.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','medium','Beta-1 selective blockers are preferred in asthmatic patients with hypertension because:','["Less bronchoconstriction than non-selective beta-blockers (though not entirely safe)","Complete lack of bronchospasm","No effect on heart rate","Only peripheral vasodilation"]',0,'Beta-1 selective blockers (atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol) have relatively less beta-2 blockade → less bronchoconstriction; still use with caution in asthma.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','hard','Which drug causes Stevens-Johnson syndrome and should be avoided in sulfa-allergic patients?','["Sulfonylureas (e.g., glipizide) and sulfonamide-based drugs","Metformin","DPP-4 inhibitors","GLP-1 agonists"]',0,'Sulfonylureas contain sulfonamide group — cross-reactivity in sulfa-allergic patients; SJS is a rare but severe ADR.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','medium','Vancomycin is used as a drug of choice for:','["MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) and C. difficile (oral)","Pseudomonas","Anaerobes","Fungal infections"]',0,'Vancomycin: glycopeptide antibiotic; IV for MRSA; oral (not absorbed) for C. difficile colitis.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','hard','Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic with a risk of:','["Agranulocytosis (requires regular CBC monitoring)","Tardive dyskinesia","QT prolongation mainly","Hyperprolactinemia"]',0,'Clozapine: superior efficacy in treatment-resistant schizophrenia; main risk = agranulocytosis (1-2%) → mandatory WBC monitoring.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','hard','ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy because they cause:','["Fetal renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, limb defects (RAAS blockade in fetus)","Placenta previa","Gestational diabetes","Preterm labour"]',0,'ACE inhibitors in 2nd/3rd trimester: fetal RAAS blockade → renal failure, oligohydramnios, Potter sequence, neonatal anuria.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','medium','The reversal agent for non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers (e.g., vecuronium) is:','["Neostigmine (anticholinesterase)","Atropine alone","Succinylcholine","Suxamethonium"]',0,'Neostigmine (+ atropine to prevent bradycardia): inhibits AChE → increased ACh at NMJ → competes out non-depolarising blocker.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','hard','The drug of choice for drug-resistant falciparum malaria is:','["Artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) e.g., artemether-lumefantrine","Chloroquine alone","Primaquine","Doxycycline alone"]',0,'Artemisinin (artesunate/artemether) + partner drug (lumefantrine/piperaquine) = ACT: first-line for uncomplicated P. falciparum globally.','WHO Guidelines',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','medium','Radioactive iodine (¹³¹I) therapy is contraindicated in:','["Pregnancy and breastfeeding","Graves'' disease in adults","Hyperthyroidism","Toxic nodular goitre"]',0,'¹³¹I: crosses placenta → fetal thyroid ablation → cretinism. Absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','medium','The mechanism of levodopa (L-DOPA) in Parkinson''s disease:','["Crosses BBB → converted to dopamine in substantia nigra (by DOPA decarboxylase)","Directly activates dopamine receptors","Inhibits MAO-B only","Increases ACh"]',0,'L-DOPA + carbidopa (DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor that doesn''t cross BBB): peripheral conversion prevented → more L-DOPA reaches brain → converted to DA.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','medium','Furosemide (loop diuretic) acts on:','["Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter (NKCC2) in thick ascending loop of Henle","NCC in DCT (thiazides)","ENaC in collecting duct (spironolactone)","AQP2 (ADH antagonists)"]',0,'Furosemide: inhibits NKCC2 in thick ascending limb of loop of Henle → most potent diuretic.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','medium','Grey baby syndrome with chloramphenicol in neonates is due to:','["Deficient hepatic UDP-glucuronyltransferase → accumulation of chloramphenicol","Renal toxicity","Bone marrow suppression","GI absorption difference"]',0,'Neonates lack UDP-glucuronyltransferase → chloramphenicol accumulates → cardiovascular collapse (grey baby syndrome) at doses >50 mg/kg/day.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','hard','The mechanism of lithium in bipolar disorder:','["Inhibits inositol monophosphatase → depletes inositol → dampens IP3/DAG signaling","Blocks D2 receptors","Inhibits MAO","Agonises 5-HT receptors"]',0,'Lithium: inhibits IMPase and IMPase → inositol depletion → PKC signaling dampened. Also inhibits GSK-3. Mechanism not fully established.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','hard','Organophosphate poisoning is treated with:','["Atropine (high dose) + pralidoxime (PAM) early","Neostigmine","Physostigmine alone","Edrophonium"]',0,'Organophosphates: irreversibly inhibit AChE → excess ACh. Rx: atropine blocks muscarinic effects; pralidoxime (2-PAM) reactivates AChE if given early (before "aging").','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','hard','Statin (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor) side effect requiring monitoring is:','["Myopathy and rhabdomyolysis (check CK); hepatotoxicity (check LFTs)","Agranulocytosis","Pulmonary fibrosis","SJS"]',0,'Statins: myalgia common, rhabdomyolysis rare but serious (elevated CK, myoglobinuria → renal failure). LFTs monitored — hepatotoxicity (class effect, rare).','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Endocrine','hard','Which insulin preparation has the longest duration of action?','["Insulin glargine (Lantus) — 24 hours, no peak (peakless)","NPH (Isophane) — 16-18 hours","Regular (soluble) — 6-8 hours","Lispro — 3-4 hours (ultra-short)"]',0,'Insulin glargine: precipitates at neutral pH of subcutaneous tissue → slow, peakless 24-hour release. Degludec lasts >42 hours.','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Antimicrobials','hard','Rifampicin is used in tuberculosis but importantly:','["Induces CYP450 enzymes → reduces efficacy of OCP, warfarin, and many drugs","Inhibits CYP450","Is bacteriostatic only","Causes pellagra"]',0,'Rifampicin: potent CYP450 inducer (CYP3A4, 2C9, etc.) → reduces plasma levels of many drugs → adjust doses; red-orange body secretions.','KD Tripathi',2024),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','CNS','medium','Morphine is contraindicated in head injury because:','["Causes CO₂ retention → vasodilation → raised ICP; also miosis clouds neurological assessment","Causes hypertension","Reduces seizure threshold","Causes agranulocytosis"]',0,'Morphine → respiratory depression → hypercapnia → cerebral vasodilation → ICP increase. Also sedation + miosis obscures neuro monitoring.','KD Tripathi',2023),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Cardiovascular','medium','Spironolactone acts as a:','["Competitive aldosterone antagonist at mineralocorticoid receptor (K⁺-sparing diuretic)","Loop diuretic","Osmotic diuretic","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"]',0,'Spironolactone: blocks aldosterone receptor in principal cells → ENaC down → Na⁺ retention blocked → K⁺ retained (K⁺-sparing).','KD Tripathi',2022),
('NEET PG','Pharmacology','Autonomic','medium','Salbutamol (albuterol) is used in asthma as a:','["Short-acting beta-2 agonist (SABA) — bronchodilator","Muscarinic antagonist","Corticosteroid","Leukotriene antagonist"]',0,'Salbutamol: selective β2-agonist → relaxes bronchial smooth muscle → bronchodilation. Onset 5-15 min, duration 4-6 hours.','KD Tripathi',2024),

-- ====== NEET PG — MICROBIOLOGY (40 questions) ======
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','easy','The Gram stain differentiates bacteria based on:','["Cell wall composition (peptidoglycan thickness)","Presence of flagella","DNA structure","Endospore formation"]',0,'Gram-positive: thick peptidoglycan → retains crystal violet → purple. Gram-negative: thin PG + outer membrane → decolorised → red (safranin counterstain).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','Mycobacterium tuberculosis stains with which special stain?','["Ziehl-Neelsen (acid-fast stain)","Gram stain","Silver stain","India ink"]',0,'MTB is acid-fast due to mycolic acid in cell wall (resists decolorization with acid-alcohol). ZN stain: red bacilli on blue background.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','The toxin of Clostridium botulinum causes flaccid paralysis by:','["Cleaving SNARE proteins → preventing ACh vesicle fusion at NMJ","Blocking ACh receptors","Stimulating ACh release","Inhibiting AChE"]',0,'Botulinum toxin: metalloprotease cleaves SNAP-25/synaptobrevin (SNARE proteins) → ACh-containing vesicles cannot fuse with presynaptic membrane → no ACh release → flaccid paralysis.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','The "rusty sputum" of lobar pneumonia is produced by:','["Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcal pneumonia)","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]',0,'S. pneumoniae: lobar pneumonia; rusty sputum = hemoglobin degradation products in alveolar exudate. Klebsiella = "currant jelly" sputum.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','easy','The HIV genome contains:','["Two copies of positive-sense ssRNA","dsDNA","dsRNA","Negative-sense ssRNA"]',0,'HIV (retrovirus): diploid positive-sense ssRNA genome + reverse transcriptase. Class VI Baltimore classification.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','medium','The Tzanck smear (showing multinucleated giant cells) is used for diagnosis of:','["Herpes simplex (HSV) and varicella-zoster (VZV)","Measles","Mumps","HIV"]',0,'Tzanck smear: vesicle roof scraped, stained with Giemsa → intranuclear inclusions + multinucleated giant cells = HSV/VZV.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','hard','Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a complication of:','["Measles virus (paramyxovirus)","Rubella","Mumps","EBV"]',0,'SSPE: rare, fatal CNS complication years after measles infection; due to defective measles virus in CNS; demyelination, intranuclear inclusions.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','medium','Candida albicans is identified by:','["Germ tube test (germ tube positive at 37°C in serum)","India ink","ZN stain","KOH mount alone"]',0,'Germ tube test: C. albicans forms germ tubes in human serum at 37°C within 2-3 hours (95% sensitivity).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','medium','Malaria parasites in RBCs are diagnosed by:','["Thick and thin Giemsa-stained blood films","Dark field microscopy","Culture","Gram stain"]',0,'Peripheral blood smear: Giemsa-stained thin film (species ID) and thick film (concentration method for increased sensitivity).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','Helicobacter pylori causes peptic ulcer disease by:','["Producing urease → NH₃ → raises local pH → survives in gastric acid; also causes mucosal inflammation","Producing toxin that kills mucosa directly only","Inhibiting acid secretion","Blocking prostaglandins"]',0,'H. pylori: urease (allows survival), CagA virulence factor, vacuolating cytotoxin (VacA) → gastric/duodenal ulcer; gastric cancer risk.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','Pseudomonas aeruginosa produces a blue-green pigment called:','["Pyocyanin (blue-green) + pyoverdine (fluorescent yellow-green)","Prodigiosin (red — Serratia)","Violacein","Chromobacterium"]',0,'P. aeruginosa produces pyocyanin (blue-green) and pyoverdine (fluorescent) → characteristic colour of infected burns/wounds.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','medium','Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) is the first marker to appear in:','["Acute HBV infection (appears 2-8 weeks after exposure, before symptoms)","HCV","HAV","HDV"]',0,'HBsAg: earliest detectable marker of HBV infection (appears before LFT elevation). Persists >6 months = chronic HBV. anti-HBs = immunity.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','hard','Aspergillus fumigatus causes invasive aspergillosis in immunocompromised patients. Its characteristic feature is:','["Septate hyphae with 45° angle branching on KOH/tissue stain","Broad non-septate (pauci-septate) hyphae at 90° (Mucor)","Yeast with narrow neck budding","Spherules (Coccidioides)"]',0,'Aspergillus: septate hyphae branching at acute 45° angles. Mucor: non-septate (pauci-septate), right-angle 90° branching.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','hard','The causative organism of leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is transmitted by:','["Phlebotomus sandfly (female)","Anopheles mosquito","Aedes mosquito","Culex mosquito"]',0,'Leishmania donovani (visceral leishmaniasis/kala-azar): transmitted by bite of female Phlebotomus sandfly (vector).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','Diphtheria toxin (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) acts by:','["Inhibiting EF-2 (elongation factor 2) by ADP-ribosylation → blocks protein synthesis","Cleaving SNARE proteins","Activating adenylate cyclase","Blocking Na⁺ channels"]',0,'Diphtheria toxin: fragment A ADP-ribosylates EF-2 → stops ribosomal translocation → inhibits protein synthesis → cell death (myocardium, nerves).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','hard','The dengue NS1 antigen test is positive:','["In the first 5 days of fever (viremic phase, before antibodies develop)","After day 7","Only in dengue hemorrhagic fever","Only in day 10+"]',0,'Dengue NS1 antigen: secreted by infected cells; detectable days 1-5 of illness; disappears as IgM rises. Useful early diagnostic marker.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','medium','The treatment of choice for cryptococcal meningitis in immunocompromised patients is:','["Amphotericin B + flucytosine (induction) → fluconazole (consolidation/maintenance)","Fluconazole alone","Itraconazole","Voriconazole"]',0,'Cryptococcal meningitis: IDSA recommends induction with liposomal AmB + 5-FC × 2 weeks → consolidation with fluconazole 400 mg × 8 weeks → maintenance.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','medium','Ascaris lumbricoides (roundworm) infection is diagnosed by:','["Stool examination for characteristic decorticated ova","Thick blood film","ZN stain","Widal test"]',0,'Ascaris: characteristic corticated (fertilised) or decorticated (infertile) ova in stool. Adults may be visible in stool/imaging.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','The Widal test detects antibodies against:','["Salmonella typhi O and H antigens (and S. paratyphi A, B)","Brucella","Leptospira","Bartonella"]',0,'Widal test: detects agglutinating antibodies to S. typhi O (LPS antigen) and H (flagellar) antigens → diagnosis of enteric fever.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','hard','The mechanism of influenza virus entry into cells involves:','["Hemagglutinin (HA) binding to sialic acid receptors → endocytosis → membrane fusion","Neuraminidase alone","Reverse transcriptase","Integration of DNA"]',0,'Influenza: HA binds to sialic acid on respiratory epithelial cells → receptor-mediated endocytosis → low pH in endosome → HA conformational change → membrane fusion → viral RNA release.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','hard','Dermatophytes are diagnosed by:','["KOH mount (potassium hydroxide preparation) of skin scrapings","Blood culture","Gram stain","VDRL"]',0,'Dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton): digest keratin. KOH dissolves keratin → hyphae/arthroconidia visible. Wood''s lamp: fluorescent M. canis.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','Which bacteria produces penicillinase (beta-lactamase) and is treated with amoxicillin-clavulanate?','["Staphylococcus aureus (penicillinase-producing strains)","MRSA (resistant to all beta-lactams)","E. coli sensitive strains","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]',0,'Penicillinase-producing S. aureus: clavulanate (beta-lactamase inhibitor) protects amoxicillin from enzymatic hydrolysis. MRSA requires vancomycin.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','hard','In Plasmodium falciparum malaria, cerebral malaria is caused by:','["Infected RBC cytoadherence to brain endothelium (sequestration via PfEMP1 — P. falciparum erythrocyte membrane protein 1)","Toxin release","Direct tissue invasion","Immune complex deposition"]',0,'P. falciparum: infected RBCs express PfEMP1 on surface → binds ICAM-1 on cerebral endothelium → sequestration → microvascular occlusion → cerebral malaria.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','medium','TORCH infections: which is most dangerous if acquired in first trimester?','["Rubella (causes congenital rubella syndrome: cataracts, deafness, CHD, PDA)","CMV in 3rd trimester","Toxoplasma in 3rd trimester","HSV during delivery"]',0,'Rubella in T1: highest risk of congenital rubella syndrome (60%). CMV: most common congenital infection overall. Toxoplasma: more severe if T1 but less frequent transmission.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','Vibrio cholerae El Tor produces rice-water diarrhoea by:','["CT (cholera toxin): ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha → constitutive adenylate cyclase activation → cAMP → Cl⁻ secretion","Cytotoxin that kills enterocytes","Invading intestinal epithelium","Producing cytotoxic necrotizing factor"]',0,'Cholera toxin B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside; A1 subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha → locked ON adenylate cyclase → massive cAMP → CFTR activation → Cl⁻/water secretion.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','medium','The drug of choice for Candida esophagitis in HIV patients is:','["Fluconazole (azole — inhibits ergosterol synthesis via CYP51 / lanosterol demethylase)","Nystatin alone (topical only)","Amphotericin B for mild cases","Terbinafine"]',0,'Candida esophagitis: systemic fluconazole 200 mg/day × 14-21 days. Nystatin for oral thrush only (not absorbed systemically).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','The CAMP test is used to identify:','["Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae)","Group A Strep","Listeria","Enterococcus"]',0,'CAMP test: GBS produces CAMP factor that synergises with S. aureus beta-hemolysin → enhanced hemolysis (arrowhead). GBS = neonatal meningitis.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','hard','HIV infects CD4+ T cells using coreceptors:','["CCR5 (macrophage-tropic R5) and CXCR4 (T-cell tropic X4)","CD8 and CCR3","CD4 alone","ACE2"]',0,'HIV gp120 binds CD4 + CCR5 (R5 strains, early infection) or CXCR4 (X4 strains, late infection/AIDS). Maraviroc blocks CCR5.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','medium','Giardia lamblia (intestinalis) causes:','["Malabsorption, steatorrhoea, and floating stools","Bloody diarrhoea","Liver abscess","Rectal prolapse"]',0,'Giardia: binds to small bowel villi (not invasive) → disrupts brush border → malabsorption → fatty, frothy, foul-smelling stools.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','The test to differentiate Staphylococcus aureus from other staphylococci is:','["Coagulase test (S. aureus is coagulase-positive)","Catalase test (all staph are positive)","Oxidase test","Urease test"]',0,'Coagulase test: S. aureus (coagulase-positive) vs S. epidermidis, S. saprophyticus (coagulase-negative). Coagulase converts fibrinogen to fibrin.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','medium','Rabies virus causes negri bodies (intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies) in:','["Purkinje cells of cerebellum and pyramidal cells of hippocampus (Ammon''s horn)","Liver","Spleen","Peripheral nerves"]',0,'Negri bodies: eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions in neurons (hippocampus, cerebellum) — pathognomonic of rabies; contain viral nucleocapsids.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','hard','Coccidioidomycosis is caused by Coccidioides immitis, endemic to:','["Southwestern USA (San Joaquin Valley) and northern Mexico","India","Africa","Southeast Asia"]',0,'Coccidioides: dimorphic fungus endemic to SW USA (California, Arizona, Texas) dry/arid regions. Inhaled arthroconidia → spherules in tissue.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','hard','The Entamoeba histolytica cyst is distinguished from E. coli cyst by:','["E. histolytica: up to 4 nuclei with eccentric karyosome; E. coli: up to 8 nuclei with eccentric karyosome","E. histolytica always has Chromatoid bodies","No difference","Nucleus number alone"]',0,'E. histolytica cyst: 1-4 nuclei, central karyosome, chromatoid bars (cigar-shaped). E. coli cyst: up to 8 nuclei, eccentric karyosome, splinter-shaped chromatoid bars.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','The urease test is used to identify:','["Helicobacter pylori (and Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella)","E. coli","Salmonella","Shigella"]',0,'Urease test: H. pylori (rapid urease test from biopsy), Proteus (strong urease — "Proteus smells like ammonia"), Klebsiella; positive = pink/red colour.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','hard','The ReoPro (abciximab) mechanism differs from direct thrombin inhibitors — but regarding virology: Poliovirus is a:','["Picornavirus — positive-sense ssRNA, naked, 30 nm, transmitted fecal-oral","Paramyxovirus","Adenovirus","Togavirus"]',0,'Poliovirus: Picornaviridae family; positive-sense ssRNA, non-enveloped; fecal-oral transmission; 3 serotypes; OPV (live) or IPV (killed) vaccine.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','medium','Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) in HIV patients is treated with:','["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX, co-trimoxazole)","Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Itraconazole"]',0,'PCP: TMP-SMX is first-line (both treatment and prophylaxis when CD4 < 200). Alternative: pentamidine, atovaquone. Note: PCP is a fungus, not protozoan.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','hard','Mechanism of tetracycline resistance:','["Efflux pump (tet genes) pumping tetracycline out of bacterial cell","Beta-lactamase production","Ribosomal methylation","Outer membrane impermeability only"]',0,'Tetracycline resistance: primarily tetracycline-specific efflux pumps (tet genes on plasmids) → active transport of tetracycline out. Ribosomal protection also seen.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Parasitology','medium','Trichomonas vaginalis is a:','["Flagellated protozoan — sexually transmitted; treated with metronidazole","Helminth","Cyst-forming protozoan","Sporozoite"]',0,'T. vaginalis: pear-shaped, flagellated protozoan; STI. Women: frothy, yellow-green vaginal discharge, strawberry cervix. Dx: wet mount (motile trophozoites).','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Bacteriology','medium','Bordetella pertussis (whooping cough) toxin acts by:','["ADP-ribosylating Gi-alpha → inhibits adenylate cyclase inhibition → cAMP increases","Inhibiting EF-2","Cleaving SNARE proteins","Blocking ACh release"]',0,'Pertussis toxin: ADP-ribosylates Gi → Gi locked OFF → no inhibition of adenylate cyclase → cAMP rises → impairs immune cell function.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2024),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Virology','medium','The vaccine for Hepatitis B is:','["Recombinant HBsAg (yeast-produced subunit vaccine — not live)","Live attenuated","Inactivated whole virus","Toxoid"]',0,'HBV vaccine: recombinant HBsAg produced in Saccharomyces cerevisiae; not live; 3-dose series (0, 1, 6 months); anti-HBs >10 mIU/mL = protective.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2023),
('NEET PG','Microbiology','Mycology','medium','India ink preparation is used for:','["Cryptococcus neoformans (capsule visible as clear halo against dark background)","Aspergillus","Dermatophytes","Candida"]',0,'India ink: Cryptococcus neoformans large polysaccharide capsule repels ink → clear halo around yeast. Also use cryptococcal antigen (latex agglutination) in CSF.','Ananthanarayan & Paniker',2022);
